membr:Badawi foaf:currentProject membr:Project_Badawi_2611021640 .

membr:Project_Badawi_2611021640_F2611021640 a rsrch:Finding ;
    RDFS:label """1. The object slot of earn is strongly restricted lexically and semantically, while the subject slot is open. Against a whole-corpus baseline, the strongest object collexemes are living (G² = 3,130), money (1,799), wage, reputation, nickname, keep, respect and profit.\r
2. Semantically, money objects occur 8.3 times more often with earn than with verbs in general (10.7 times at the level of the money-and-business field).\r
3. Semantically, money objects occur 8.3 times more often with earn than with verbs in general (10.7 times at the level of the money-and-business field).\r
4. The money reading is preferred with I (G² = 43.9; where G² is the log-likelihood), you (G² = 19.3) and she (G² = 10.1), not with he, they, we or it.\r
Overall, the construction is  partially schematic: open in its subject, partially lexicalised in its object, with earn a living as its most strongly associated sub-pattern.\r
""" ;
    rsrch:basedOn cx:engLME_EarnConstruction .

references:34c997b5-ef0f-4a6b-9acb-63f34659318d evid:biblio "Barteld, F. & Ziem, A. (2020). Construction mining: Identifying construction candidates for the German constructicon. Belgian Journal of Linguistics, 34:5–16." .

references:3e049438-dc56-4ccf-81f4-ca59b7f455cd evid:biblio "Forsberg, M., Johansson, R., Bäckström, L., Borin, L., Lyngfelt, B., Olofsson, J., & Prentice, J. (2014). From construction candidates to constructicon entries: An experiment using semi-automatic methods for identifying constructions in corpora. Constructions and Frames, 6(1):114–135." .

references:46ceab2b-78ca-4369-b397-1f4cfc77b173 evid:biblio "Evert, S. & Heid, U. (2019). Between collocation and construction: Lexical preferences in non-idiomatic word combinations. Presentation at EUROPHRAS 2019: Productive Patterns in Phraseology, Santiago de Compostela, Spain." .

references:51b4d46e-301b-44fe-bd25-95050b325e05 evid:biblio "Dunn, J. (2024). Computational Construction Grammar: A Usage-Based Approach. Elements in Cognitive Linguistics. Cambridge University Press." .

references:d99b0509-98a6-4dd2-b8f9-ca4e25d49072 evid:biblio "Diessel, H. (2019). The Grammar Network: How Linguistic Structure Is Shaped by Language Use. Cambridge University Press." .

references:f1506d5b-dc68-44ec-aad3-3a3cc3f0a081 evid:biblio "Goldberg, A. E. (1995). Constructions. A Construction Grammar Approach to Argument Structure. Chicago: The University of Chicago Press." .

membr:Project_Badawi_2611021640 a rsrch:Project ;
    rsrch:hasFindings membr:Project_Badawi_2611021640_F2611021640 ;
    rsrch:projectName "How can the constructionhood of verb-argument patterns be measured from corpus data along fixedness, productivity and association, and how do these dimensions behave across the subsumption lattice of a verb’s patterns?" .

